// A handle may name an instance of a generic Sysl type, and each type argument is a boundary type
// that is one Sysl type: a collection is not, nor a class, nor a parenthesized type that is not a
// tuple; and the brackets hold at least one argument and close at the end.
// error: `Vector[int]` cannot be a type argument of the Sysl type a handle is on: each is one Sysl type, an integer of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, `byte`, `real`, `char`, `bool`, `unit`, `string`, or an `Option`, a `Result` or a tuple of these
// error: `Card` cannot be a type argument of the Sysl type a handle is on: each is one Sysl type, an integer of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, `byte`, `real`, `char`, `bool`, `unit`, `string`, or an `Option`, a `Result` or a tuple of these
// error: `(int)` cannot be a type argument of the Sysl type a handle is on: each is one Sysl type, an integer of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, `byte`, `real`, `char`, `bool`, `unit`, `string`, or an `Option`, a `Result` or a tuple of these
// error: `@sysl` on a class names the Sysl type it is a handle on, by its module and name, as in `sysl.checksum.Crc32`, or an instance of a generic one, as in `sysl.buf.Buf[int]`; `pkg.views.Signal[]` is not one
// error: `@sysl` on a class names the Sysl type it is a handle on, by its module and name, as in `sysl.checksum.Crc32`, or an instance of a generic one, as in `sysl.buf.Buf[int]`; `pkg.views.Signal[int` is not one

class Card

@sysl("pkg.views.Signal[Vector[int]]")
class Numbers

@sysl("pkg.views.Signal[Card]")
class Cards

@sysl("pkg.views.Signal[(int)]")
class Parenthesized

@sysl("pkg.views.Signal[]")
class Empty

@sysl("pkg.views.Signal[int")
class Unclosed

main() = ()
